The '''2015 FIFA Club World Cup''' (officially known as the '''FIFA Club World Cup Japan 2015 presented by Alibaba E-Auto''' for sponsorship reasons)<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/y=2015/m=12/news=alibaba-e-auto-signs-as-presenting-partner-of-the-fifa-club-world-cup-2743056.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151211021029/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/y=2015/m=12/news=alibaba-e-auto-signs-as-presenting-partner-of-the-fifa-club-world-cup-2743056.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=11 December 2015 |title=Alibaba E-Auto signs as Presenting Partner of the FIFA Club World Cup |website=FIFA.com |date=9 December 2015}}</ref> was the 12th edition of the [[FIFA Club World Cup]], a [[FIFA]]-organised international club [[association football|football]] tournament between the winners of the six continental confederations, as well as the host nation's league champions.<ref name="regulations">{{cite web |url=http://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/70/28/09/fcwcreg2015en_neutral.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151126173136/http://resources.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/70/28/09/fcwcreg2015en_neutral.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=26 November 2015 |title=Regulations – FIFA Club World Cup Japan 2015 |publisher=FIFA}}</ref> The tournament was hosted by Japan between 10 and 20 December 2015.<ref name="hosts">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/about-fifa/news/y=2015/m=4/news=japan-set-to-host-continental-champions-in-2015-and-2016-2593526.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150425065733/http://www.fifa.com/about-fifa/news/y=2015/m=4/news=japan-set-to-host-continental-champions-in-2015-and-2016-2593526.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=25 April 2015 |title=Japan set to host continental champions in 2015 and 2016 |website=FIFA.com |date=23 April 2015}}</ref>

Defending champions [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] did not qualify as they were eliminated in the semi-finals of the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League]]. The eventual winners of that competition, [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]], went on to win a record third Club World Cup title, beating [[Guangzhou F.C.|Guangzhou Evergrande]] 3–0 in the semi-finals before beating [[Club Atlético River Plate|River Plate]] by the same margin in the [[2015 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]].

==Host bids==
The application process for the 2015–2016 as well as the 2017–2018 editions, i.e. two hosts, each hosting two years, began in February 2014.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/newsid=2248101/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131219233246/http://www.fifa.com//clubworldcup/news/newsid=2248101/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=19 December 2013 |title=Blatter: A legacy for the future |website=FIFA.com |date=19 December 2013}}</ref> Member associations interested in hosting had to submit a declaration of interest by 30 March 2014, and provide the complete set of bidding documents by 25 August 2014.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/organisation/news/newsid=2293621/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140306142058/http://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/organisation/news/newsid=2293621/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=6 March 2014 |title=Bidding process opened for eight FIFA competitions |website=FIFA.com |date=19 December 2013}}</ref> The [[FIFA Executive Committee]] was to select the hosts at their meeting in [[Morocco]] in December 2014.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://zeenews.india.com/sports/football/india-may-get-to-host-fifa-club-world-cup_1485299.html |title=India may get to host FIFA Club World Cup |website=India.com |date=15 October 2014}}</ref> However, no such decision regarding the 2015–2016 host was made until 2015.

The following countries expressed an interest in bidding to host the tournament:<ref>{{cite web |title=High interest in hosting FIFA competitions |url=https://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/organisation/news/newsid=2336227/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140510175305/http://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/organisation/news/newsid=2336227/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=10 May 2014 |website=FIFA.com |date=9 May 2014}}</ref>
*{{fba|India}} (withdrew interest in November 2014)<ref>{{cite web |title=India not bidding for 2015 and 2016 Fifa Club World Cups |url=http://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/sports/football/top-stories/India-not-bidding-for-2015-and-2016-Fifa-Club-World-Cups/articleshow/45136849.cms |work=The Times of India |date=13 November 2014 |access-date=20 January 2015}}</ref>
*{{fba|Japan}}

Japan was officially confirmed as the host of the 2015 and [[2016 FIFA Club World Cup|2016]] tournaments on 23 April 2015.<ref name="hosts"/>

==Proposed change to format==
Under a proposed change to the competition, led by the [[Oceania Football Confederation]], the new format would mean a removal of the knockout rounds for the quarter-finals and play-off round, replacing it with two [[Round-robin tournament|round-robin]] groups of three teams, consisting of two teams from the host nation and the champions of AFC, CAF, CONCACAF, and OFC, with the group winners advancing to the semi-finals to play the champions of CONMEBOL and UEFA. This would allow for all teams to play at least two matches, avoiding the current situation where the loser of the play-off round would play only one match.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/news/article.cfm?c_id=4&objectid=11366502 |title=Football: More matches likely in Club Cup |work=[[New Zealand Herald]] |date=30 November 2014 |access-date=3 December 2014}}</ref> However, this proposal was not implemented.<ref name="hosts"/>

